        "content": "**Prompt:** *Carefully read the following six sources on the practice of Daylight Saving Time (DST). Synthesize at least three sources into a coherent, well-written essay that argues a clear position on whether the United States should permanently abolish or maintain Daylight Saving Time.*\n\n---\n\n### **Student Model Essay (Scored 1-4-1 = 6/6 by AP Grader)**\n\n**[Paragraph 1: Introduction & Complex Thesis]**  \nTwice each year, millions of Americans engage in a century-old ritual: manipulating clocks by a single hour under the nostalgic guise of energy conservation and lengthened summer twilights. First instituted during the industrial mobilization of World War I to conserve coal reserves, Daylight Saving Time (DST) has since mutated into an obsolete bureaucratic vestige that exacts severe tolls upon human physiology, public safety, and domestic productivity. Although proponents contend that extended evening sunlight provides psychological leisure benefits and commercial retail stimuli (Source B), the empirical realities of circadian disruption, spike in vehicular fatalities, and negligible electrical savings mandate that the United States permanently eliminate the biannual clock transition in favor of permanent standard time.\n\n*Grader Commentary (Thesis 1/1):*  \n*Earns the thesis point. Identifies the historical context and presents a clear, qualified, defensible claim with a distinct line of reasoning.*\n\n---\n\n**[Paragraph 2: Public Health & Circadian Disruption]**  \nThe most catastrophic consequence of the biannual time shift resides in the biological vulnerability of the human circadian rhythm. While advocates frequently dismiss a lost hour of sleep as a minor inconvenience, neurobiological evidence indicates that shifting internal cellular clocks by sixty minutes triggers acute cardiovascular and neurocognitive shock. According to epidemiological data compiled by the American Academy of Sleep Medicine, the Monday immediately following the spring transition witnesses an alarming $24\\%$ surge in acute myocardial infarctions (heart attacks) nationwide (Source D). This physiological crisis is mirrored on American asphalt, where sleep-deprived commuters generate an estimated $6\\%$ jump in fatal vehicular collisions during the same week (Source A). As sleep researcher Dr. Matthew Walker observes, the human body cannot simply \"reset\" its master metabolic pacemakers on legislative command (Source D); thus, clinging to an arbitrary temporal mandate directly imperils public health for the sake of an antiquated calendar convention.\n\n*Grader Commentary (Evidence & Commentary 4/4):*  \n*Exemplary synthesis. Puts Source D and Source A into active conversation, interpreting statistical evidence with insightful analytical warrants.*\n\n---\n\n**[Paragraph 3: Rebutting the Energy Conservation Myth]**  \nBeyond physiological devastation, the foundational historic rationale for Daylight Saving Time—namely, substantial energy conservation—has been thoroughly debunked by modern municipal data. Proponents frequently cite Benjamin Franklin’s 18th-century musings on candle conservation or World War I factory output to justify the tradition (Source F). However, comprehensive billing research conducted across Indiana following its statewide DST adoption demonstrated that while household lighting demands dipped slightly, residential air-conditioning consumption spiked dramatically during extended, sweltering summer evenings, resulting in a net $1\\%$ INCREASE in total household electricity costs (Source C). Source C’s empirical billing analysis directly repudiates the outdated Department of Transportation projections cited in Source E, demonstrating that in an air-conditioned 21st-century economy, prolonged evening daylight expends far more power than it preserves.\n\n*Grader Commentary (Synthesis & Refutation):*  \n*Superb critical thinking. Directly pits Source C against Source E and Source F, exposing methodological flaws in historical arguments.*\n\n---\n\n**[Paragraph 4: Conclusion & Broader Societal Significance]**  \nUltimately, the debate over Daylight Saving Time exposes a broader societal unwillingness to dismantle obsolete institutional rituals that no longer serve human flourishing. While boutique golf courses and recreational retailers benefit marginally from extended evening commerce (Source B), a democracy cannot ethically trade human lives and cardiovascular health for incremental merchant revenues. By abandoning the chaotic biannual transition and permanently locking the nation into Standard Time, Congress has the opportunity to align American civic life with biological reality, proving that rational public welfare can triumph over institutional inertia.\n\n*Grader Commentary (Sophistication Row C 1/1):*  \n*Earns the sophistication point. Situates the debate within a broader philosophical discussion of public welfare vs commercial interest, maintaining vivid, elevated, persuasive prose throughout.*"
