        "heading": "5. Complete Scored 6/6 Student Model Rhetorical Analysis Essay (FRQ 2) with Annotations",
        "content": "**Prompt:** *The following passage is an excerpt from Florence Kelley’s speech delivered before the convention of the National American Woman Suffrage Association in Philadelphia on July 22, 1905. Read the passage carefully. Write an essay that analyzes the rhetorical choices Kelley makes to convey her message about child labor.*\n\n---\n\n### **Student Model Essay (Scored 1-4-1 = 6/6 by AP Grader)**\n\n**[Paragraph 1: Exigence & Chronological Thesis]**  \nAt the turn of the twentieth century, the rapid acceleration of American industrialization produced immense corporate fortunes at the direct expense of society’s most vulnerable citizens: impoverished working-class children. Barred from the ballot box as a disenfranchised woman, progressive reformer Florence Kelley addressed the 1905 National American Woman Suffrage Association with an urgent moral exigence. Confronting an audience of privileged women who possessed civic influence but lacked the vote, Kelley employs biting juxtaposition, rhythmic parallel syntax, and inclusive collective guilt to expose the grotesque inhumanity of child labor and galvanize her affluent female listeners into active legislative agitation.\n\n*Grader Commentary (Thesis 1/1):*  \n*Earns thesis point. Explicitly grounds analysis in Kelley's specific historical exigence, identifying distinct rhetorical moves and connecting them to her ultimate purpose.*\n\n---\n\n**[Paragraph 2: The Grievous Juxtaposition of Childhood and Exploitation]**  \nKelley opens her address by establishing a stark, haunting contrast between the pastoral ideal of childhood and the brutal reality of factory servitude. She strategically recites cold legislative statistics, observing that while men enjoy statutory eight-hour workdays, little girls in Georgia textile mills \"stand upon their feet, through the weary night... working eleven hours at a stretch.\" By counterpoising the tender physical fragility of young girls with the grueling, adult burdens of industrial machinery, Kelley strips industrial manufacturing of its veneer of economic progress. The image of children working \"while we sleep\" is repeated with devastating cadence, transforming a passive biological necessity (sleep) into an uncomfortable reminder of moral complacency. Kelley does not allow her listeners to view child labor as a distant southern anomaly; rather, by contrasting the children's sleepless labor with her audience's serene domestic slumber, she psychologically unmasks the comfortable luxury of the affluent as direct beneficiaries of pediatric sweat.\n\n*Grader Commentary (Evidence & Commentary 4/4):*  \n*Exceptional textual integration. Kelley's choices are not merely cataloged; the student thoroughly analyzes the psychological impact upon the intended audience.*\n\n---\n\n**[Paragraph 3: Shifting from Dissonance to Complicity and Moral Duty]**  \nHaving dismantled the moral legitimacy of child labor, Kelley pivots from detached observation to direct collective implication through inclusive pronouns. Rather than chastising her audience with accusatory finger-pointing, Kelley deliberate links herself to them, repeatedly invoking \"we,\" \"our,\" and \"ours.\" She observes that the low-cost clothing and luxurious stockings worn by the women in the convention hall were woven by the bleeding fingers of children: *\"We buy; and do by our buying indirectly set up the system.\"* Through this deft commercial calculation, Kelley transforms her listeners from passive spectators of social injustice into active economic patrons of child abuse. The parallel listing of consumer garments acts as a physical inventory of guilt, forcing every woman in the room to look down at her own attire and recognize her complicity.\n\n*Grader Commentary (Line of Reasoning):*  \n*Traces the chronological evolution of Kelley's speech. Demonstrates how the author pivots from emotional indignation to personal moral accountability.*\n\n---\n\n**[Paragraph 4: Conclusion & Enfranchisement Synthesis]**  \nUltimately, Kelley’s rhetorical triumph lies in her synthesis of the anti-child-labor crusade with the overarching fight for female suffrage. Concluding her address, she reminds her listeners that while women currently \"stand without the vote,\" they possess immense moral and economic power as primary household consumers. By urging them to leverage their collective consumer boycotts and petition legislators, Kelley elevates child labor from a localized labor dispute into a universal test of American moral character. Her impassioned polemic demonstrates that true citizenship is measured not merely by the legal casting of a ballot, but by the relentless moral defense of those too small to defend themselves.\n\n*Grader Commentary (Row C Sophistication 1/1):*  \n*Demonstrates a sophisticated, nuanced understanding of Kelley's dual purpose (child labor reform AND women's suffrage mobilization), maintaining an elevated academic voice from first sentence to last.*"
